# ENDO02B — Explain v3 ("Learn" depth): hyperglycaemic crises — DKA vs HHS, pathogenesis and precipitants, definition and grading, mimics (alcoholic and starvation ketosis),
# acidosis and ketones through an episode, fluid deficits and fluids, potassium, insulin and how to stop it, bicarbonate, phosphate, cerebral oedema, pregnancy, euglycaemic DKA, HHS.
# No mnemonics or hooks. Driven by the real questions in qpack/ENDO02B.md (52 links: DKA-07 mimics 6; DKA-03 pathogenesis and DKA-13 potassium 5 each; DKA-06 grading,
# DKA-19 cerebral oedema and DKA-21 euglycaemic DKA 4 each; DKA-08, -14, -20 and -22 never asked).
# Fields as TOX01, plus SPINE, PRIMER and step. Table cards (DKA-01, -06, -11) take parts a, b, c… in the order of the card’s rows. Bullet cards (DKA-03, -05) take a, b, c… in bullet order.
# Figures checked online on 22 Nov 2026:
#  - ADA/EASD/JBDS/AACE/DTS consensus 2024 (Umpierrez et al.; Diabetologia 67:1455, the twin of Diabetes Care dci24-0032), read via PMC11343900: DKA = glucose ≥200 mg/dL (11.1) or known diabetes,
#    β-hydroxybutyrate ≥3.0 mmol/L (or urine ketones ≥2+), pH <7.3 and/or HCO3 <18; euglycaemic DKA (glucose <200) about 10%; severity mild pH 7.25–7.30 or HCO3 15–18, moderate 7.0–7.25 or 10–<15,
#    severe <7.0 or <10 (BHB >6), ward / step-down / ICU; inpatient DKA mortality 0.20% (type 1) to 1.04% (type 2), HHS 5–10 times higher; mixed DKA/HHS 27% of US admissions (DKA 38%, HHS 35%),
#    overlap in more than a third; infection 14–58% of DKA worldwide, insulin omission 41–60% in US series, infection 30–60% of HHS; fluids 500–1000 mL/h of saline or balanced crystalloid over
#    the first 2–4 h, deficits over 24–48 h, 250 mL boluses in the old or those with heart or kidney failure; balanced crystalloids associated with faster resolution and less hyperchloraemic acidosis;
#    0.45% saline only if osmolality is not falling; 1.6 mmol/L Na per 100 mg/dL glucose; at glucose <250 add 5–10% dextrose and cut insulin to 0.05 U/kg/h, keep glucose about 200 until resolution;
#    fixed-rate 0.1 U/kg/h with optional 0.1 U/kg bolus; basal insulin 0.15–0.3 U/kg alongside the infusion; SC rapid-acting analogues 1–2-hourly for mild/moderate DKA only; IV short-acting
#    insulin preferred; HHS 0.05 U/kg/h (0.1 if BHB ≥3, pH <7.3 or HCO3 <18), glucose fall ≤90–120 mg/dL/h, osmolality fall 3–8 mOsm/kg/h; K replaced below 5.0 at 20–30 mmol/L to keep 4–5,
#    K <3.5 → 10 mmol/h and delay insulin, K 2 h after insulin then 4-hourly, K ≤2.5 about threefold mortality; bicarbonate only if pH <7.0, 100 mmol 8.4% in 400 mL water 2-hourly;
#    phosphate not routine, 20–30 mmol potassium phosphate when weakness or cardiorespiratory compromise; resolution BHB <0.6 and venous pH ≥7.3 or HCO3 ≥18, not anion gap or urine ketones;
#    HHS resolution osmolality <300, glucose <250, urine >0.5 mL/kg/h, cognition improved; SC overlap 1–2 h, 0.5–0.6 U/kg/day if insulin-naive (0.3 if high hypoglycaemia risk), home dose otherwise;
#    hyperchloraemic NAGMA after treatment is common and self-limiting; nitroprusside measures acetoacetate, not BHB; cerebral oedema rare in adults, mortality about 30%; hypoglycaemia <40 mg/dL
#    4.8-fold mortality; LMWH prophylaxis; DKA in up to 2% of pregnancies with pre-existing diabetes; SGLT2i DKA about 4% in type 1, stop on admission, dextrose from the start in euglycaemic DKA.
#  - ADA 2006 consensus (Kitabchi, Diabetes Care 29:2739), read: first-hour 15–20 mL/kg (1–1.5 L); bolus 0.1 U/kg then 0.1 U/kg/h; fall 50–75 mg/dL/h; dextrose at 200 (DKA) / 300 (HHS);
#    K started below 5.3 if urine about 50 mL/h, insulin delayed until K >3.3; bicarbonate 100 mmol in 400 mL with 20 mEq KCl over 2 h if pH <6.9; phosphate for cardiac dysfunction, anaemia,
#    respiratory depression or PO4 <1.0 mg/dL, overzealous use → hypocalcaemia, no benefit; resolution glucose <200, HCO3 ≥18, pH >7.3; IV insulin 1–2 h after first SC dose; 0.5–0.8 U/kg/day;
#    cerebral oedema 0.7–1.0% of children; about 20% of HHS without known diabetes; normal anion gap 7–9 with current analysers.
# Second review (22 Nov 2026), read online: ADA 2006 consensus Table 1 via scispace PDF (water 100 mL/kg DKA, 100–200 mL/kg HHS; 6 L vs 9 L); ADA 2009 text via slideshare copy
#    (0.14 U/kg/h without bolus; K replaced below 5.0–5.2; insulin held until K >3.3; phosphate if <1.0 mg/dL, maximum 4.5 mmol/h = 1.5 mL/h K2PO4; bicarbonate at pH <6.9, 100 mmol in 400 mL
#    with 20 mEq KCl at 200 mL/h for 2 h; dextrose at 200 (DKA) / 300 (HHS), insulin then 0.02–0.05 U/kg/h; glucose kept 150–200 (DKA) / 250–300 (HHS); 0.5–0.8 U/kg/day; Morris-type RCT of
#    21 patients at pH 6.9–7.1); ADA 2009 flowchart as reproduced by WikiDoc (10% first-hour rule → 0.14 U/kg bolus; K bands 3.3 / 5.2; HHS glucose 200–300); 2024 consensus PMC11343900
#    re-read (severity table with BHB 3–6 / >6; phosphate ‘<1.0 mmol/l’ with muscle weakness such as respiratory or cardiac compromise, 20–30 mmol KPO4; K <3.5 → 10 mmol/h; K ≤2.5 threefold
#    mortality; 0.05 U/kg/h and glucose about 200 at <250 in DKA; HHS fall ≤90–120 mg/dL/h; adult cerebral oedema <0.1%, mortality about 30%; up to 2% of pregestational pregnancies;
#    SGLT2 DKA about 4% in type 1; euglycaemic about 10%; 0.45% only if osmolality not falling, in HHS); ISPAD 2022 chapter 11 PDF (cerebral injury 0.5–0.9%, mortality 21–24%; mannitol
#    0.5–1 g/kg over 10–15 min; 3% saline 2.5–5 mL/kg over 10–15 min; hypocapnia, high urea, bicarbonate as risk factors; PECARN FLUID 1389 episodes, no difference in mental status or
#    cerebral injury; insulin 1 h after fluids, no bolus; severe pH <7.1 or HCO3 <5); JBDS HHS quick-reference guide (glucose 10–15 mmol/L in 24 h, fall ≤5 mmol/L/h, Na fall ≤10 mmol/L
#    in 24 h, osmolality 3–8 mOsm/kg/h, insulin 0.05 U/kg/h once glucose stops falling, at once if BHB >1); Mayo Clinic review (fetal mortality 10–35% in DKA in pregnancy).
# Not re-read: JBDS 2023 DKA targets (BHB fall ≥0.5 mmol/L/h, HCO3 rise ≥3 mmol/L/h), Glaser 2001 NEJM itself, Katz 1.6 / Hillier 2.4, and the 2024 HHS dextrose threshold (only in a figure).

SPINE = [
  ("How much insulin is left", "Absolute insulin lack with high glucagon turns fat into ketoacids within a day (DKA), while a little residual insulin blocks ketogenesis but not hyperglycaemia, so water loss runs on for days (HHS)."),
  ("Defining and grading the crisis", "DKA needs all three of hyperglycaemia or known diabetes, ketones of 3 mmol/L or more and acidaemia below pH 7.3 or bicarbonate 18; HHS is glucose of 600 or more with effective osmolality over 300 and no significant ketoacidosis."),
  ("Ketoacids, their tests and their mimics", "β-hydroxybutyrate is the main ketoacid and the one the urine dipstick misses, a normal glucose with ketoacidosis means alcohol, starvation, pregnancy or an SGLT2 inhibitor, and a normal-gap acidosis late in treatment is chloride, not failure."),
  ("Fluid first, then potassium", "The first hour is isotonic fluid, the next choice is set by the corrected sodium, and insulin waits until potassium is at least 3.5 because total-body potassium is low however high the serum level."),
  ("Insulin until the ketones clear", "A fixed 0.1 U/kg/h IV infusion runs until ketones are below 0.6 with pH 7.3 or bicarbonate 18, dextrose is added at glucose 250 so it can continue, and it stops only after subcutaneous insulin has overlapped for 1–2 hours."),
  ("Bicarbonate, phosphate and complications", "Bicarbonate is for pH below 7.0 only, phosphate only for severe deficiency with weakness, and the treatment complications to expect are hypokalaemia and hypoglycaemia in adults and cerebral oedema in children."),
]

PRIMER = "Insulin does two separate jobs, and they need very different amounts of it: a small amount is enough to stop fat breaking down into free fatty acids, while much more is needed to move glucose into muscle and stop the liver making it. Glucagon, cortisol, catecholamines and growth hormone push the other way, so the balance between insulin and these counter-regulatory hormones, not the absolute glucose, decides the metabolic state. Once plasma glucose exceeds the kidney’s threshold of about 180 mg/dL, glucose spills into the urine and drags water, sodium and potassium with it. Figures in this deck follow the 2024 ADA/EASD/JBDS/AACE/DTS consensus where it has changed things, with the older ADA 2009 numbers the examiners still key shown beside them."

